Hi,
basically, jax always complains when you use .at[...] with boolean masks inside of jax.jit, because boolean masks refer to a non-static number of elements.
However, in many cases, these operations are well-defined even in the context of static shapes.
Currently, it is possible to achieve this with jnp.where but not with .at[...]:
import jax
import jax.numpy as jnp

def f(x):
    return x.at[x < 5].set(0)

def g(x):
    return jnp.where(x < 5, 0, x)

x = jnp.arange(10)
assert jnp.array_equal(f(x), g(x))  # type: ignore

print(jax.jit(g)(x))  # no error
print(jax.jit(f)(x))  # error
One could say "if this behavior is needed, just use jnp.where" - but with more complex indexing, jnp.where becomes less and less intuitive.
Consider the following example:
def f(x):
    return x.at[:4, jnp.mod(x[0], 2) == 0].set(0)  # <-- far more intuitive (atleast for me)

def g(x):
    return x.at[:4].set(jnp.where(jnp.mod(x[0], 2) == 0, 0, x[:4]))  # <-- very abstract and unintuitive (atleast for me)

x = jnp.reshape(jnp.arange(100), shape=(10, 10))
assert jnp.array_equal(f(x), g(x))

print(jax.jit(g)(x))  # no error
print(jax.jit(f)(x))  # error
PS:
By the way, why is type: ignore needed in the first example code? The type checker complains here.
